                  %mi dx
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} o(dx)
            and hence has vanishing integral. But with the naive integral, it does not.
          %h3#the_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus The fundamental theorem of calculus
          %p
            The naive integral satisfies a fundamental theorem of calculus for all cogerm forms: for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi c
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_c \mathrm{d}\omega = \langle \omega| c_b\rangle - \langle \omega | c_a \rangle.
          %p
            This follows directly from the definition of the naive integral and the cogerm differential
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}
          %p
            The genuine integral does not satisfy as general a fundamental theorem of calculus as the naive integral. It does, however, satisfy FTC for differentials of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi c
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_c \mathrm{d}f = f(c(b)) - f(c(a))
          %p This can be proven in exactly the same way as the usual FTC for (line) integrals.
          %p
            The restriction of FTC to differentials of functions is fairly natural if we recognize that FTC is a special case of the generalized
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes%27+theorem"} Stokes' theorem
            %em exterior
            differentials of forms, not the commutative cogerm differential. It just so happens that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a function, then the exterior differential of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            regarded as a 0-form agrees with the cogerm differential of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            regarded as a cogerm 1-form.
          %h3#existence_of_integrals Existence of integrals
          %p The existence of naive integrals is fairly easy to verify, since they are just defined in terms of ordinary 1-variable integrals. But the existence of genuine integrals is rather less obvious. Here we prove that they exist for at least one reasonably general class of forms.
          %p
            Recall that a
            %em 1-cojet differential 1-form
            is a cogerm 1-form that depends only on the 1-jet of a curve, i.e. its value and its tangent vector. A 1-cojet form is equivalently just a function (not necessarily linear) on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
